WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.640
Our parents form a big part of our personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.840
during the time when we&nbsp;
completely depend on their care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:17.520
This happens because as young children we perceive&nbsp;
them as almighty, endlessly wise, and flawless.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.400
We also believe that whatever they&nbsp;
do, literally - whatever - they - do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.840
they do out of unconditional love And&nbsp;
because we think they are infallible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.000
we consequently think that we are&nbsp;
at fault whenever a conflict arises.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.400
In order to reduce any kind of conflict,&nbsp;
we develop patterns of behavior, feelings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.960
and thinking designed to please our&nbsp;
parents and minimise any friction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.840
Unfortunately, these patterns can lead to&nbsp;
a personality that is misaligned with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:02.560
true spiritual self. And so, we grow into&nbsp;
adults that behave in ways that make us sad.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.080
The Hoffman Process was designed to help us detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.440
negative patterns of behavior, break them&nbsp;
and to become the person we really are.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:22.560
To condense a lifetime of analysis into seven&nbsp;
days, the process is highly structured, very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:29.440
intense, and applies a multitude of techniques.&nbsp;
Afterward, participants often understand why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.960
they are the way they are and learn to let go&nbsp;
of the negative sides of their personality.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:43.200
To understand how the process works, let us look&nbsp;
at three types of people who on the outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.040
look just fine, but on the&nbsp;
inside, deeply struggle.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.680
Eva is 27, a university graduate, and&nbsp;
she has just started her first job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:02.160
She’s unable to trust men and is at a loss&nbsp;
when it comes to forming healthy relationships.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:12.160
Jay is 51, an overachiever, and a respected CEO.&nbsp;
However, his marriage is broken and his two adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:18.160
children hardly ever call. He asks himself&nbsp;
if that’s really all that life has to offer?

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:27.840
Tom is 45, married with one child, but he’s got no&nbsp;
friends. He lives with his family far outside town&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:35.840
and signs up for the process when he realizes that&nbsp;
his daughter also has problems making friends.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.640
Through the process Eva will realise that, when&nbsp;
her dad left when she was 4, and her mother then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:49.520
struggled as a single mom and a lonely woman,&nbsp;
it had a big impact on her. Over the years her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:55.280
own relationship with her father became broken&nbsp;
and she learned that men couldn’t be trusted.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:04.000
Jay will learn that his life was going great&nbsp;
up until his little brother was born — a funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:11.680
extraverted, and bright boy. Afterwards, Jay was&nbsp;
hardly ever noticed. Jay soon realizes that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.840
parents would only pay attention to him if he&nbsp;
excelled in school or sports, causing him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.760
grow into an adult who tries to be the best in&nbsp;
everything, in order to get the love he seeks.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:32.640
Tom will realize that he was an accident — born&nbsp;
to a young couple who had just begun dating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.560
As a young boy he was often neglected,&nbsp;
because their life was difficult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.720
His parents who were overwhelmed by the situation&nbsp;
would often snap at him over the smallest thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.600
Little Tom began to think that he was the cause&nbsp;
of all misery — he should never have been born.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:00.080
The process begins with a phone call from a&nbsp;
designated therapist. This is followed by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:06.080
50-page questionnaire that helps their client&nbsp;
to reflect on their lives. Going through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:11.840
questions, the participants identify particular&nbsp;
patterns in behavior and link them to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.800
parents. Once returned, the therapist uses the&nbsp;
questionnaire to understand the underlying issues.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:28.800
On day 1, all three arrive at the retreat, a place&nbsp;
isolated from the outside world. They hand over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:35.760
their phones and any books to ensure there are no&nbsp;
distractions. Tom then meets his therapist for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:43.360
in-depth conversation. Afterwards, he gets to know&nbsp;
the other 17 participants and learns that everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:49.520
carries within them an inner child that manages&nbsp;
their expectations, thoughts, and feelings.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.160
On day 2 Jay and the others&nbsp;
learn more about their parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.200
and how their behavioral patterns have formed&nbsp;
out of love for their father and mother.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.080
Jay, who only got real attention&nbsp;
when he impressed his parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.240
could never be good enough to&nbsp;
get all the love he needed.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.400
Such conditional love is&nbsp;
negative because subconsciously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.160
Jay takes on the blame for&nbsp;
this disjunction and develops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.840
a ‘core shame belief’ — he begins to think&nbsp;
something is inherently wrong with himself.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.680
And so, even after 50 years of&nbsp;
trying so hard to be the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.880
his inner child is still looking for the&nbsp;
unconditional love he got so little of.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.600
On day 3, Eva realizes how the patterns she&nbsp;
established as a young girl subconsciously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:50.720
project into her adult relationships — anyone&nbsp;
who reminds Eva of her father, can’t be trusted.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.280
Through a group game later that day, she&nbsp;
experiences that there are men she can rely on.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:06.480
Eva then accuses her parents of making her the&nbsp;
victim of their broken relationship - this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.840
important because this way she can retrace&nbsp;
her negative pattern back to her parents.&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.000
Afterwards, she learns to understand their story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.800
because they too were once children,&nbsp;
with parents who had their own issues.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.840
This is important because then she&nbsp;
can understand their situation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.560
realizes that the way she was brought up&nbsp;
was all her parents were able to provide.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.640
Now she can make peace with her past,&nbsp;
and her mistrust of men begins to wither.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:45.520
On day 4, participants say goodbye to their&nbsp;
parents and their childhood and ask themselves:&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.320
Where am I from?
What injuries do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.240
I bring along from my mother and father?
How have these injuries impacted my life?&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:02.160
What strategies have I developed to&nbsp;
compensate for these injuries so far?

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.120
Jay, who didn’t want to be reminded of the&nbsp;
painful thought that he’s not worthy of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:13.760
parents unconditional love, compensated by only&nbsp;
contacting them when he had outstanding news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:18.240
such as another promotion. Knowing&nbsp;
that they would then surely listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.480
and act predictably, he reduces&nbsp;
the risk of feeling hurt.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.120
After this realization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.320
Jay creates a development plan to follow&nbsp;
his vision for life after the process.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:39.840
Day 5 is about our vindictiveness. Tom, who&nbsp;
didn’t want to be reminded that he is the root of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:47.280
all problems, compensated for the pain he&nbsp;
experienced by hiding himself. At a young age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.400
children keep their honor by thinking of how to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.320
one day, pay their parents back&nbsp;
for the misery they caused them.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:08:00.000
As we mature, such vindictive&nbsp;
thoughts move into the subconscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.320
and by the time he is a teenager, Tom begins&nbsp;
to pay back. First by breaking the law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:12.320
then by breaking all contacts&nbsp;
with his friends and family.

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.680
On this day, Tom stops feeling vindictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:22.240
and makes peace with his parents. His inner&nbsp;
child regains his honor and grows in strength.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:28.640
Grown-up Tom can now leave his hiding place&nbsp;
and finally enjoy meeting other people.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:37.040
On day 6 the participants get to know the&nbsp;
saboteur, the inner voice that warns us of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:44.320
any change in order to keep us in the safe old&nbsp;
world we know so well. Eva hears the voice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:50.480
whenever she opens up to the opposite sex&nbsp;
— it whispers “remember, you can’t trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:58.080
men”. Tom hears it when he enters a group of&nbsp;
people — “you are not welcome here”. Whenever Jay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:04.720
tries to relax and be himself, the saboteur says&nbsp;
“Don’t just do nothing! You are better than this!”

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:13.680
After getting to know this evil agent, they learn&nbsp;
how to deal with this enemy of our progress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:21.840
Then the group recaps the entire process&nbsp;
and celebrates their new found selves.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.160
On day 7 they learn practical tools on&nbsp;
how to cope with the change they will face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:33.360
when they go back into their lives.&nbsp;
Finally, they go home to their parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.400
to complete what they have started,&nbsp;
end their old lives and begin afresh.

00:09:40.698 --> 00:09:46.218
To learn more about the Hoffman process, or to&nbsp;
read the research on it by the Harvard Center for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.218 --> 00:09:52.138
Public Leadership, check out the descriptions&nbsp;
below. Prior to creating this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.138 --> 00:09:57.418
our founder went through the Hoffman Process&nbsp;
and it had a profound effect on his life.

